                </td>
                <td width=572 valign=center colspan=10 style="width:343.6000pt;padding:0.0000pt 5.4000pt 0.0000pt 5.4000pt ;border-left:none;
mso-border-left-alt:none;border-right:1.0000pt solid windowtext;mso-border-right-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;
border-top:none;mso-border-top-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;border-bottom:1.0000pt solid windowtext;
mso-border-bottom-alt:0.5000pt solid windowtext;">
                    <p class=MsoNormal><span style="font-family:仿宋;mso-bidi-font-family:'Times New Roman';font-size:12.0000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><font face="仿宋"><p>Medical Microbiology is a compulsory basic course for clinical medicine, stomatology, basic medicine, and forensic medicine majors. It mainly includes five aspects: basic bacteriology, clinical bacteriology, basic virology, clinical virology, and pathogenic fungi. Basic bacteriology and virology mainly discuss the morphological structure, growth and reproduction, genetic variation, pathogenicity and immunity, laboratory diagnosis, and specific prevention and treatment of bacteria and viruses; clinical bacteriology and virology focus on describing the biological characteristics, pathogenicity and immune response, microbiological examination, and prevention and treatment of pathogenic bacteria and viruses related to medicine; pathogenic fungi mainly discuss the main characteristics of fungi and their relationship with medicine.</p></font></span><span style="font-family:'Times New Roman';mso-fareast-font-family:仿宋;font-size:10.5000pt;
mso-font-kerning:1.0000pt;"><o:p></o:p></span></p>
                </td>
            </tr>
